   "**A double-tap attacks the act of rescue, and the act of rescue is the only thing a state can still do for its own people in a village like this** — police, medics and neighbours arriving at a known site within minutes; the direct effect is that the next strike on a village produces a second wave of dead professionals instead of saved civilians; the compound consequence is that rescue itself becomes a calculated risk, taken by people who now know the calculation, and the queue at the next aid distribution forms more slowly and thins out — the village is de-populated by the fear of the second bomb, not by the first",
